page("how-to-use-a-compact-charcoal-grill","How to Use a Compact Charcoal Grill (Start to Finish)","Setup, lighting, heat zones and pack-down for a small charcoal grill, step by step on the Slot Grill Compact.",IMG["c28"],f"""
<p>A compact charcoal grill rewards a little planning. You have less room than on a big grill, so where you put the coals matters more. Here's the whole routine on the Slot Grill Compact, from flat pack to cleanup.</p>
<h2>1. Pick a safe spot</h2><p>Set up on a stable, level, non-combustible surface: bare soil, gravel, sand, concrete, or a fire-safe pad. Keep clear of tents, vehicles, dry grass and anything that can burn. Never use a charcoal grill indoors, in a garage, tent or vehicle. Burning charcoal makes carbon monoxide.</p>
<h2>2. Assemble it (about 30–60 seconds)</h2><ol><li>Hold one end piece with the <b>SLOT GRILL</b> text facing out.</li><li>Slide the two tabs of the two-tab side panel into its slots.</li><li>Add the second end piece, text facing out. The end pieces are universal.</li><li>Set the remaining side panel's top tabs onto the end pieces and slide its three bottom tabs into the first panel. The sides lock into a tight V.</li><li>Check every tab is fully seated and the grill sits level.</li></ol>
<h2>3. Light the charcoal</h2><p>A chimney starter is the cleanest way: fill it, light paper underneath, and pour when the top coals are ashed over. On a grill this size you rarely need a full chimney. Skip lighter fluid; it can taste and it flares in wind.</p>
<h2>4. Build two zones</h2><p>With a 16.25" × 9" cooking area, the easiest trick is to bank most coals at one end. That gives you a hot sear zone and a cooler end to move food that's flaring or done. It's the single biggest upgrade for small-grill cooking.</p>
<h2>5. Set the grates and cook</h2><p>Lay the two grill grates on top, or swap one for a <a href="{GRID}">griddle plate</a> so you can sear on one side and do eggs or onions on the other. Use an <a href="{THERMO}">instant-read thermometer</a> instead of guessing.</p>
<h2>6. Pack it flat</h2><p>Let everything cool completely. Dump cold ash, lift off the grates, slide out the V panel, pull the end pieces, and stack. The Compact packs to about 1" thick. If you leave it set up between cooks, just pull one side panel once the ash is cold, empty it, and slide it back in.</p>
""")
page("how-to-season-a-compact-grill","How to Season a Carbon Steel Compact Grill","Season raw carbon steel once at home so your small grill cooks cleaner and resists rust. Oven method, oils and upkeep.",IMG["balm"],f"""
<p>The Slot Grill Compact is raw 12-gauge carbon steel. Like a cast-iron pan, it does best with a seasoned coat: a thin layer of oil baked on until it polymerizes and bonds to the steel. Do it once before your first cook and you're set.</p>
<h2>What you need</h2><ul><li>A food-safe oil with a high smoke point, or <a href="{BALM}">Slot Grill Seasoning Balm</a></li><li>Paper towels or a scrap of cotton</li><li>An oven (or a hot fire in the grill itself)</li></ul>
<h2>Steps</h2><ol><li>Wash the pieces with hot water and dish soap and dry them completely.</li><li>Wipe on a <b>very thin</b> coat. It should look almost dry, a light sheen. Too much oil turns sticky and streaky.</li><li>Bake at 400°F for 1 hour (the Seasoning Balm instructions). Let it cool.</li><li>Repeat 2–3 times to build the layer.</li></ol>
<blockquote>"I coated the entire grill in safflower oil, placed the assembled grill in the oven and let it bake for an hour at 450°. The grill has a smooth, non-stick surface."<cite>Richard Shapero, Compact owner</cite></blockquote>
<h2>The body vs. the grates</h2><p>Slot Grill recommends high-temperature BBQ paint as a good option for the grill body, or the balm, and keeping the grilling surfaces lightly oiled. Never use BBQ paint on cooking surfaces or grates.</p>
<h2>If you see rust</h2><p>Light surface rust is cosmetic. Scrub it off with a cheap wire mesh pad, dry, re-oil, and heat. Thick steel shrugs that off for years.</p>
""")
page("compact-grill-tailgate-guide","Tailgating With a Compact Charcoal Grill","How to run a tailgate on a small charcoal grill: packing list, setup on gravel or pavement, menu timing and cleanup.",IMG["both16"],f"""
<p>A tailgate grill has three jobs: fit in the truck with the coolers, set up fast in a crowded lot, and feed people without babysitting. The Compact packs about 1" thick and weighs about 14 lb, so it rides behind a seat or under a cooler.</p>
<h2>Where to set it up</h2><p>Not on the tailgate itself. Put it on pavement or gravel next to the truck, on a stable, level, non-combustible surface. The 2⅞" air gap under the coal bed helps minimize radiant heat to the surface below, but it's still a charcoal fire. Check your stadium's lot rules; many require grills to be a set distance from vehicles and ban open flames in garages.</p>
<h2>Packing list</h2><ul><li>Slot Grill Compact (and a griddle plate for breakfast or smash burgers)</li><li>Chimney starter, charcoal, lighter</li><li>Long tongs, spatula, heat-safe gloves</li><li>Instant-read thermometer</li><li>A metal can with a lid for cold ash</li><li>Foil pan for resting meat, trash bags, paper towels</li></ul>
<h2>Menu timing for a small grill</h2><p>Plan in waves. The 146 sq in surface does sausages or brats first (they hold well in a foil pan), then burgers, then anything quick like peppers and onions on the griddle. Two-zone coals keep the waves going without the fire getting away from you.</p>
<blockquote>"We got this for my husband for Father's Day and he loved it. Commented on how well it was built and is making plans to use it on the tailgate at the jobsites."<cite>Kimi Hines, Compact owner</cite></blockquote>
<h2>Leaving the lot</h2><p>Never pack hot steel or ash into a vehicle. Let the coals burn down, drown or dispose of them where the venue allows, and stack the grill once it's cool. Old gloves help, steel and soot go together.</p>
""")
page("compact-grill-camping-guide","Camping With a Compact Charcoal Grill","Car camping, RV and campsite cooking on a small charcoal grill: fire rules, packing, and why it doubles as a mini fire pit.",IMG["c13"],f"""
<p>Shared campground grills can be anything from rusted to used as a trash burner. Bringing your own small grill means you know what's been cooked on it. The Compact is sized for one to three people and packs flat into a camp box, trunk, kayak or RV storage.</p>
<h2>Before you go</h2><ul><li>Check the campground's rules and any burn bans. Some allow charcoal only in fire rings.</li><li>Season the grill at home (<a href="/how-to-season-a-compact-grill.html">how</a>).</li><li>Pack charcoal in a dry bag; damp charcoal is miserable to light.</li></ul>
<h2>At camp</h2><p>Set up on bare soil, gravel or sand, away from tents and dry brush. It takes about a minute to assemble by hand, with no loose hardware to drop in the dirt. Where fires are allowed, the same grill works as a small fire pit after dinner for marshmallows.</p>
<blockquote>"This morning we wanted to roast marshmallows. Set up was efficient. In minutes we were roasting our sweet breakfast."<cite>Richard Shapero, RV owner</cite></blockquote>
<h2>Honest tradeoffs</h2><p>Thick steel is heavier than a flimsy folding grill. At about 14 lb the Compact is for car, truck, boat and RV camping, not backpacking. And carbon steel needs to be kept dry and lightly oiled after trips or it will surface-rust.</p>
<h2>Cleanup</h2><p>Let the ash go completely cold, put it in a metal container, and follow the park's disposal rules. Wipe the grates with a little oil before you pack up.</p>
""")
page("two-zone-fire-on-a-small-grill","How to Build a Two-Zone Fire on a Small Charcoal Grill","The one technique that makes small grills easy: hot side, cool side, and how to use them for steaks, chicken and burgers.",IMG["c08"],f"""
<p>On a big grill you can get away with an even bed of coals. On a 16.25" × 9" surface, an even bed means everything cooks at one speed: too fast. A two-zone fire fixes that.</p>
<h2>Set it up</h2><ol><li>Pour lit coals into one end of the firebox, roughly two-thirds of the length.</li><li>Leave the last third thin or empty.</li><li>Put the grates on and let them heat for a few minutes.</li></ol>
<h2>How to use it</h2><ul><li><b>Sear</b> over the coals, then slide to the cool end to finish thicker cuts.</li><li><b>Flare-ups</b>: move the food to the cool end until they die down. No water bottle needed.</li><li><b>Holding</b>: finished burgers or sausages wait at the cool end while the next batch cooks.</li></ul>
<h2>With a griddle</h2><p>The Compact's two-piece top lets you run a grate over the hot side and a <a href="{GRID}">griddle plate</a> over the other. Steaks on the grate, onions or eggs on the flat top, one fire.</p>
<p>Use a thermometer for doneness. USDA minimum internal temperatures: 145°F for whole cuts of beef, pork and lamb (with a 3-minute rest), 160°F for ground meats, 165°F for poultry.</p>
""")
page("best-charcoal-for-small-grills","Charcoal vs. Wood on a Small Grill: What to Burn","Briquettes, lump charcoal or cooking wood on a compact grill: how each behaves and how much you need.",IMG["c28"],f"""
<p>The Compact burns charcoal or cooking wood. Each behaves a little differently in a small firebox.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th></th><th>Briquettes</th><th>Lump charcoal</th><th>Cooking wood</th></tr><tr><td>Heat</td><td>Steady, even</td><td>Hotter, faster</td><td>Varies, needs to burn down</td></tr><tr><td>Burn time</td><td>Longer</td><td>Shorter</td><td>Shortest; add as you go</td></tr><tr><td>Ash</td><td>More</td><td>Less</td><td>Some</td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>Burgers, chicken, longer cooks</td><td>Steak sears</td><td>Flavor, campfire-style cooks</td></tr></table></div>
<h2>How much?</h2><p>A half chimney of briquettes is plenty for a quick weeknight cook on this size grill. Add a few fresh pieces to the hot side if you're doing waves for a group.</p>
<h2>Never burn</h2><p>Treated or painted lumber, trash, or unknown materials. Use only real charcoal or untreated cooking wood.</p>
""")
page("how-to-clean-and-store-a-compact-grill","How to Clean and Store a Compact Grill","Quick between-cook cleanup, deep cleaning, and storing a flat-pack carbon steel grill so it doesn't rust.",IMG["both02"],f"""
<p>Flat panels are the easiest grill parts in the world to clean. Here's the routine Slot Grill recommends.</p>
<h2>Between cooks</h2><p>If you leave it assembled (under a carport or at a permitted campsite), you don't need a full clean every time. Once the ash is completely cold, pull one side panel, empty it, and slide the panel back in.</p>
<h2>Deep clean</h2><ol><li>Let it cool completely and dump the ash into a metal container.</li><li>Wash with hot water, dish soap and a cheap wire mesh scouring pad (about $1.50).</li><li>Dry every piece completely.</li><li>Wipe a light coat of oil or <a href="{BALM}">Seasoning Balm</a> on the grilling surfaces.</li></ol>
<h2>Storing it</h2><p>Stack it flat, about 1" thick, somewhere dry. One owner keeps theirs under a bunk at work; others keep it in an overland trailer or RV closet. A bag or old box keeps soot off your gear.</p>
<blockquote>"Used it the first weekend we received it. Worked great, easy cleanup and it easily stored in our overland trailer."<cite>Verified Compact owner</cite></blockquote>
""")
def recipe(serves,time,ingr,steps,tip=""):
    return f'<p><b>Serves:</b> {serves} · <b>Time:</b> {time} · <b>Grill:</b> Slot Grill Compact</p><h2>Ingredients</h2><ul>'+"".join(f"<li>{i}</li>" for i in ingr)+'</ul><h2>Steps</h2><ol>'+"".join(f"<li>{s}</li>" for s in steps)+"</ol>"+(f'<p class="note">{tip}</p>' if tip else "")
page("reverse-sear-steak-small-grill","Ribeye on a Small Charcoal Grill: Sear and Slide","A thick ribeye cooked on a compact charcoal grill using a two-zone fire. Hard sear, gentle finish.",IMG["c08"],recipe("2","30 min",["2 ribeyes, about 1¼\" thick","Coarse salt and black pepper","Butter (optional)"],["Salt the steaks 40 minutes ahead (or right before).","Bank a full chimney's worth of lit coals at one end.","Sear over the hot side 2–3 minutes per side until well crusted.","Slide to the cool end and flip every couple of minutes until an instant-read thermometer reads your target. USDA minimum for beef steaks is 145°F with a 3-minute rest; many cooks pull rare-to-medium earlier by preference.","Rest a few minutes, top with butter, slice."],"Two thick steaks fill the Compact nicely. For four, cook in two rounds and hold the first at the cool end."))
page("smash-burgers-on-a-griddle","Smash Burgers on a Small Charcoal Griddle","Crispy-edged smash burgers on a carbon steel griddle plate over charcoal.",IMG["pl2"],recipe("2–3","25 min",["1 lb 80/20 ground beef, in 3 oz balls","Salt and pepper","American cheese","Buns, thin-sliced onion, pickles"],["Set a griddle plate over a hot, even bed of coals and let it get properly hot.","Add a little oil. Place 2–3 balls and smash hard with a stiff spatula. Season.","Cook until the edges are deeply browned, scrape and flip, add cheese.","Toast buns on the grate side.","Ground beef should reach 160°F. Thin patties get there fast."],f'Needs the <a href="{GRID}">griddle add-on</a> ($20 for one Compact griddle).'))
page("chicken-thighs-small-charcoal-grill","Charcoal Chicken Thighs on a Compact Grill","Juicy bone-in chicken thighs on a small grill, using the cool side to cook through without burning.",IMG["c13"],recipe("2–3","45 min",["6 bone-in, skin-on chicken thighs","Your favorite rub","BBQ sauce (optional)"],["Rub the thighs well.","Build a two-zone fire. Start skin-side up on the cool end.","Cook, turning occasionally, until close to done.","Move to the hot side to crisp the skin; watch for flare-ups.","Sauce at the very end if you like. Chicken must reach 165°F; thighs are better closer to 175°F."],"Thighs forgive a lot, which makes them perfect for learning a new grill."))
page("campfire-breakfast-griddle","Camp Breakfast on a Compact Grill: Bacon, Eggs and Potatoes","A full camp breakfast on one small charcoal fire using a griddle plate and grate.",IMG["pl1"],recipe("2","35 min",["6 slices bacon","4 eggs","1 large potato, diced and par-cooked at home","Salt, pepper, hot sauce","Tortillas (optional)"],["Light a modest chimney and spread the coals under a griddle plate.","Cook the bacon first and leave the fat on the griddle.","Fry the potatoes in the fat until crisp.","Push to the cool edge, fry the eggs.","Warm tortillas on the grate side."],"Leftover coals make the morning campfire."))
page("steak-fajitas-compact-grill","Steak Fajitas on a Compact Charcoal Grill","Skirt steak on the grate, peppers and onions on the griddle, one small fire.",IMG["both16"],recipe("3","40 min",["1–1½ lb skirt steak","Lime, garlic, cumin, chili powder, salt","2 bell peppers and 1 onion, sliced","Tortillas, salsa, avocado"],["Marinate the steak in lime, garlic and spices for 30 minutes or more.","Hot fire under the grate, griddle plate on the other half.","Peppers and onions on the griddle with a little oil.","Sear the skirt steak hard, 2–4 minutes per side; beef steaks to at least 145°F with a rest.","Slice thin across the grain and serve."]))
page("compact-vs-full-size-slot-grill","Slot Grill Compact vs. Full Size: Which One?","Side-by-side specs and who each Slot Grill size is for: cooking area, weight, steel gauge and price.",IMG["both02"],f"""
<p>Same tab-and-slot design, two sizes. The Compact is the Full Size at 80% scale.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th></th><th>Compact</th><th>Full Size</th></tr><tr><td>Cooking area</td><td>16.25" × 9" (146 sq in)</td><td>20.5" × 11.25" (231 sq in)</td></tr><tr><td>Footprint</td><td>17.5" × 10.25"</td><td>21.75" × 12.75"</td></tr><tr><td>Height</td><td>10.5"</td><td>13"</td></tr><tr><td>Steel</td><td>12-gauge carbon</td><td>10-gauge carbon</td></tr><tr><td>Weight (grill only)</td><td>~14 lb</td><td>~27.6 lb</td></tr><tr><td>Packs flat</td><td>~1"</td><td>~1.25"</td></tr><tr><td>Air gap</td><td>2⅞"</td><td>3⅝"</td></tr><tr><td>Price (grill only)</td><td>$89.97</td><td>$149.97</td></tr></table></div>
<h2>Pick the Compact if</h2><p>You cook for one to three, want the lightest heavy-duty option, or need to save space in a trunk, kayak, RV or camp box.</p>
<h2>Pick the Full Size if</h2><p>You cook for a family or group, want room for cast iron and big cuts, and travel by truck, UTV or boat.</p>
<blockquote>"I love both of my slot grills but the compact is better for road trips, especially for just two people and just the right cooking space."<cite>Verified owner</cite></blockquote>
{productbox("full")}
""")
page("real-slot-grill","How to Know You're Getting a Real Slot Grill","Genuine Slot Grills are thick Texas-made carbon steel with SLOT GRILL cut into the end pieces, sold only through official @SlotGrill channels.",IMG["c07"],f"""
<p>Because Slot Grill videos have been seen by millions, there are copies out there using the name and photos. A real one is easy to check:</p>
<ul><li><b>Steel:</b> 12-gauge (Compact) or 10-gauge (Full Size) carbon steel. Not thin stamped sheet metal that flexes.</li><li><b>Weight:</b> about 14 lb (Compact) or 27.6 lb (Full Size), grill only.</li><li><b>Name in the steel:</b> SLOT GRILL is cut into the end pieces.</li><li><b>Price:</b> $89.97 Compact, $149.97 Full Size. A heavy steel grill for under $40 isn't real.</li><li><b>Made in Texas:</b> by Precision Cut Metals LLC; ships from the shop in 1–3 days.</li><li><b>Where to buy:</b> only SlotGrill.com, the @SlotGrill TikTok Shop, and @SlotGrill on the Shop app. Slot Grill has no authorized third-party resellers.</li></ul>
<p>Slot Grill® is a registered U.S. trademark of Precision Cut Metals LLC (Reg. No. 8,180,923, registered March 17, 2026; first use August 14, 2024).</p>
<p>Unsure about a listing? Send it through the <a href="{CONTACT}">Slot Grill contact form</a> before you buy.</p>
""")
